{
  "image_name": "3d79b2e80709e44db0a3a9d8193cf88e.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how mesenchymal stromal cells contribute to the tumor microenvironment. Bone marrow–derived MSCs (BM-MSCs) and local tissue MSCs are shown as sources of stromal cells that are recruited toward tumors, apparently through chemokines such as CXCL12 and CXCL16 and through signals from tumor-derived exosomes. Immune cells also influence BM-MSCs via inflammatory cytokines, including IL-1β and TNF.\n\nAt the center, BM-MSCs appear to convert into tumor-associated MSCs/cancer-associated fibroblasts (TA-MSCs/CAFs), although the figure marks some uncertainty in this transition. Once established, these stromal cells support tumor progression in several ways. They send factors back to tumor cells, including VEGF, neuregulin 1, and BMPs, suggesting enhanced tumor growth or survival. They also promote angiogenesis through mediators such as VEGF, IL-6, and CXCL12.\n\nA major theme is immune modulation: TA-MSCs/CAFs release chemokines including CCL2, CCL7, and CCL12, which recruit myeloid-derived suppressor cells, macrophages, and monocytes. Overall, the figure depicts a feed-forward network in which tumor, immune, and stromal cells communicate to build a pro-tumor, inflammatory, and vascularized niche.",
